# AppsFlyer Capacitor plugin development

## Architecture overview

This is a **Capacitor native plugin** that wraps `@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin` — a shared,
schema-generated RPC core also used by the React Native and Cordova AppsFlyer plugins. As of
7.0.2 this plugin no longer hand-declares methods, param types, or per-method native
implementations: every method, its params, and its wire mapping to native come from
`js-core-plugin`'s generated `AppsFlyerSDK` class and `rpc-map.js`. There is no web
implementation; all methods target native platforms.

The whole bridge is one generic call in each direction:

1. JS calls `AppsFlyer.someMethod(params)` on the `AppsFlyerSDK` instance (from `js-core-plugin`).
2. `AppsFlyerSDK` resolves `someMethod` against `rpc-map.js` to get the platform's real wire
   method name and param shape, then calls `CapacitorTransport.call(method, params)`.
3. `CapacitorTransport` (`src/capacitor-transport.ts`) JSON-encodes `{method, params}` and calls
   the single native bridge method `executeRpc({requestJson})`.
4. Native (`AppsFlyerPlugin.swift` / `AppsFlyerPlugin.kt`) forwards `requestJson` verbatim to the
   platform's RPC handler (`AppsFlyerRPCBridge`/`AppsFlyerRpcHandler`, which live in the native
   AppsFlyer SDK, not this repo) and normalizes the response into a `{success, data|error}`
   envelope — **the two platforms' envelope shapes must stay in sync**, see the comments at the
   top of each `executeRpc` implementation.
5. Persistent listeners (session-ready, conversion, deep link) flow back over one shared event
   channel, `rpcEvent`, not a per-listener native event name.

### File layout

| Layer | Path | Language |
| --- | --- | --- |
| RPC transport (JSON envelope over `executeRpc`) | `src/capacitor-transport.ts` | TS |
| Non-generated enums (`AFPurchaseType`, `MediationNetwork`) | `src/constants.ts` | TS |
| Plugin entry — constructs `AppsFlyerSDK`, re-exports everything | `src/index.ts` | TS |
| Generated method/param types, RPC dispatch table (owned by `js-core-plugin`, do not hand-edit) | `node_modules/@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin/dist/generated/*` | TS/JS |
| iOS bridge (single `executeRpc` method) | `ios/Plugin/AppsFlyerPlugin.swift` | Swift |
| iOS AppDelegate-facing deep link/launch-options facade | `ios/Plugin/AppsFlyerAttribution.swift` | Swift |
| Android bridge (single `executeRpc` method) | `android/src/main/java/capacitor/plugin/appsflyer/sdk/AppsFlyerPlugin.kt` | Kotlin |
| Example app | `examples/CapacitorReact/`, `examples/qa-test-app/` | Ionic React / vanilla TS |

## Adding a new API method

New RPC methods are added to `@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin` itself (a separate package), not
hand-written here. To pick up a new/changed method in this plugin:

1. Bump the `@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin` dependency in `package.json` to the version that
   ships the method.
2. Verify `node_modules/@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin/dist/generated/rpc-map.js` and
   `methods.d.ts` contain it — nothing in this repo needs to change for a method whose shape the
   generic `executeRpc` dispatch already handles.
3. **Documentation**: update `docs/API.md` (method table + per-method reference) and any
   relevant doc under `docs/`.
4. **Example app**: add a working call in `examples/qa-test-app/src/main.ts` or
   `examples/CapacitorReact/src/App.tsx` so the method is exercised end-to-end.

Only touch `AppsFlyerPlugin.swift`/`.kt` if a method needs platform glue the generic RPC dispatch
can't express (e.g. `AppsFlyerAttribution`'s pre-bridge-ready buffering for cold-start deep
links) — not for ordinary param-in/param-out methods.

### Platform-only methods

Platform availability is data, not code: a method with no entry for a platform in `rpc-map.js`
(`android: null` or `ios: null`) rejects on that platform automatically — there is no
`call.unavailable()` to write per method.

## Coding conventions

### TypeScript

- Don't hand-write parameter interfaces for RPC methods — they're generated in
  `js-core-plugin`'s `dist/generated/methods.d.ts` and re-exported via `export * from
  '@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin'` in `src/index.ts`.
- `src/constants.ts` is the only place for hand-written exports: enums `js-core-plugin` doesn't
  provide (`AFPurchaseType`, `MediationNetwork`).
- The plugin is registered as `'AppsFlyerPlugin'` in `src/capacitor-transport.ts` via
  `registerPlugin`.
- Linting: `@ionic/eslint-config/recommended` + `@ionic/prettier-config`. Run `npm run lint` to
  check, `npm run fmt` to fix.

### Swift (iOS) / Kotlin (Android)

- Both platforms expose exactly one bridged method, `executeRpc`, which forwards raw
  `requestJson` to the native SDK's own RPC handler and normalizes the response. Don't add
  per-method `@objc`/`@PluginMethod` functions for ordinary RPC calls — that logic no longer
  exists on either platform.
- `AppsFlyerAttribution.swift` is the one piece of hand-written iOS glue: it buffers
  `handleOpen`/`continueUserActivity`/`handleLaunchOptions` calls that can arrive before the
  Capacitor bridge (and therefore the RPC channel) is ready.
- SwiftLint: `@ionic/swiftlint-config`. Run `npm run swiftlint -- lint`.

## Deprecation pattern

Method-level deprecation lives upstream in `@appsflyer-sdk/js-core-plugin`'s schema — this repo
picks it up on the next dependency bump. Deprecate this repo's own surface (the plugin entry
point, `src/constants.ts` exports) the normal way: `@deprecated` JSDoc tag with a replacement
note, plus a call-out in `docs/API.md`'s migration table.

## Event listeners

All persistent listeners (session-ready, conversion data, deep link) flow over one shared native
event channel, `rpcEvent` (`src/capacitor-transport.ts`), not a per-listener callback name.
`AppsFlyerSDK` (in `js-core-plugin`) demultiplexes incoming events by their `type` field and
dispatches to the right registered callback (`onConversionDataSuccess`, `onDeepLinking`, etc.).
Don't add new native `notifyListeners(...)` call sites for new listener types — add the event
type to `js-core-plugin`'s schema instead.

## Deep link handling (iOS)

`AppsFlyerAttribution` is a singleton that queues deep links arriving before the Capacitor bridge
(and RPC channel) is ready, flushing them once `AppsFlyerPlugin` reports the bridge is up. Don't
bypass this mechanism; deep links that arrive before the RPC channel is ready will be lost
otherwise.

## Versioning

Version numbers are tracked in **multiple places** and must stay in sync:

- `package.json`: `version`, `iosSdkVersion`, `androidSdkVersion`, `androidPluginBridgeVersion`
- `ios/Podfile` / `Package.swift`: `AppsFlyerRPC` pod/SPM version (must match `iosSdkVersion`)
- `android/build.gradle`: `af_sdk_version` (sourced from `package.json`'s `androidSdkVersion`)

CI scripts in `.github/workflows/scripts/` handle updates automatically during release branch
creation. Don't manually edit version numbers across files; use the `updatePlugin.sh`
orchestrator.

## Branching and workflow

- **`main`**: production branch. Merges into `main` trigger the release workflow.
- **`develop`**: integration branch for ongoing work.
- **Feature branches**: `dev/DELIVERY-<ticket>/description` (from `develop`).
- **Release branches**: `releases/<major>.x.x/<major>.<minor>.x/<major>.<minor>.<patch>` (created by CI via `create-release-branch-workflow.yml`).

When creating a branch locally, follow: `DELIVERY-<ticket-number>-<description>`.

## Build and verify

| Command | What it does |
|---------|-------------|
| `npm run build` | Clean + TSC + Rollup (produces `dist/`) |
| `npm run verify` | Builds iOS, Android, and web in sequence |
| `npm run verify:ios` | `pod install` + `xcodebuild` |
| `npm run verify:android` | `./gradlew clean build test` |
| `npm run lint` | ESLint + Prettier check + SwiftLint |
| `npm run fmt` | Auto-fix lint issues |
| `npm run docgen` | Regenerate API docs in README |

Always run `npm run build` and `npm run lint` before pushing.

## Release process

The plugin uses a six-stage RC release pipeline aligned with [`appsflyer-mobile-plugin-tooling`](https://github.com/AppsFlyerSDK/appsflyer-mobile-plugin-tooling) contracts. See [`docs/RELEASE_USER_MANUAL.md`](mdc:docs/RELEASE_USER_MANUAL.md) for the operator guide and [`.cursor/skills/rc-release/SKILL.md`](mdc:.cursor/skills/rc-release/SKILL.md) for the stage→workflow map.

Brief flow:

1. **RC-PREP** — Maintainer dispatches `rc-release.yml` with version inputs (`X.Y.Z-rcN`). CI cuts a release branch like `releases/<X>.x.x/<X>.<Y>.x/<X.Y.Z>-rcN`, runs `updatePlugin.sh`, pushes.
2. **RC-CI / RC-E2E** — `lint-test-build.yml`, `ios-e2e.yml`, `android-e2e.yml` run in parallel. `pre-publish-gate` requires all three green.
3. **RC-PUBLISH** — `rc-release.yml` runs `npm publish --tag QA` (or `--dry-run` if `dry_run=true`), opens a PR to `main`, creates a prerelease tag, sends a Slack notification.
4. **RC-SMOKE** — `rc-smoke.yml` synthesizes `examples/qa-test-app-rc-smoke/` from the canonical QA app pinned to the registry RC, runs the smoke plan, and posts an `rc-smoke/npm` check on the release-branch HEAD.
5. **RC-PROMOTE** — Operator labels the PR `pass QA ready for deploy`. `promote-release.yml` verifies `rc-smoke/npm` is green, strips `-rcN`, pushes the strip commit.
6. **RC-RELEASE** — On merge to `main`, `production-release.yml` dispatches `npm-publish-oidc.yml`, which runs `npm publish --provenance --access public` via OIDC trusted publishing; the parent workflow creates the GitHub release and sends Slack/Jira notifications.

Always update `CHANGELOG.md` before applying the promote label.

## Example app

The example app at `examples/CapacitorReact/` is an Ionic React + Capacitor project. It uses env vars `REACT_APP_AF_DEV_KEY` and `REACT_APP_AF_APP_ID` from `.env`. 

- `npm run installLocal` installs the plugin from the repo root (`../../`) and syncs Capacitor.
- Always keep the example app working with the latest plugin API; new methods should have a callable button or demonstration.
